WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.035
Hi, I'm Neymar O'Donnor, and this is Module 3 of OS

2
00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:04.720
Work.

3
00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:09.075
In Module 2, you saw chat, work, and desktop, three ways

4
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.460
to work with AI.

5
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.735
Now, the AI starts working in a folder on your computer

6
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.260
through the terminal.

7
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.135
It may sound like something for programmers, but we'll go slowly,

8
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.500
one command at a time.

9
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.655
By the end of the module, you'll open the terminal and

10
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.595
know where you are, install codecs from the official source, sign

11
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:31.715
in with your account without leaving any password in sight, open

12
00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.175
the agent in the right folder, ask first for a reading

13
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.315
and then for a small change and check what changed.

14
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.215
Denise and Lucia are still with us.

15
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.555
Each lesson has a hands-on practice on the computer with training

16
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:46.155
files.

17
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:49.615
When I ask, pause the video and do it right then.

18
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:52.015
From now on the AI works in a folder on your

19
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:52.815
computer.

20
00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:55.395
And if you don't know which folder you are in, it

21
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:56.475
doesn't know either.

22
00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.515
Many errors that look like AI failures are just the wrong

23
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:00.235
folder.

24
00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.882
The terminal is a window where you type a command and

25
00:01:02.907 --> 00:01:05.322
read the answer and it already comes with your computer.

26
00:01:05.347 --> 00:01:09.562
On the Mac, Lucia pressed C minor and space, typed terminal

27
00:01:09.587 --> 00:01:10.907
and hit enter.

28
00:01:11.027 --> 00:01:12.142
It took 10 seconds.

29
00:01:12.167 --> 00:01:16.102
On Linux, just look for terminal in your list of apps.

30
00:01:16.127 --> 00:01:19.462
The commands in this course are written in Bash, the language

31
00:01:19.487 --> 00:01:22.342
of the terminal on Linux and MacOS.

32
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:26.262
On Windows, they work inside WSL, which comes from Microsoft itself

33
00:01:26.287 --> 00:01:27.402
and opens as Ubuntu.

34
00:01:27.427 --> 00:01:31.162
The terminal that comes with Windows uses a different language, so

35
00:01:31.187 --> 00:01:34.287
don't paste the course commands into it without adapting them.

36
00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:37.827
A school computer with no administrator password?

37
00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:40.522
Don't force it, ask whoever manages it.

38
00:01:40.547 --> 00:01:43.982
When it opens, the terminal shows a short line ending in

39
00:01:44.007 --> 00:01:45.227
a dollar sign.

40
00:01:45.507 --> 00:01:49.442
Or on the Mac, a percent sign, with the cursor blinking.

41
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:51.487
That's the computer waiting for you.

42
00:01:51.927 --> 00:01:54.942
Denise opened the terminal for the first time and waited for

43
00:01:54.967 --> 00:01:55.782
something to happen.

44
00:01:55.807 --> 00:01:57.502
Nothing happened.

45
00:01:57.527 --> 00:01:59.062
Because it was waiting for her.

46
00:01:59.087 --> 00:02:02.528
You type the command, press enter, the answer shows up below,

47
00:02:02.553 --> 00:02:04.588
and the dollar sign comes back.

48
00:02:04.613 --> 00:02:06.608
It's always that back and forth.

49
00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:07.888
The first command is parties.

50
00:02:07.913 --> 00:02:11.868
You know this cool map with the you are here sticker?

51
00:02:11.893 --> 00:02:13.468
Pound plays that role.

52
00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:15.948
It answers with the full path of the folder you are

53
00:02:15.973 --> 00:02:16.428
in.

54
00:02:16.453 --> 00:02:17.948
Read it like an address.

55
00:02:17.973 --> 00:02:19.908
Each slash separates a folder.

56
00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:21.953
And the last one is where you are.

57
00:02:22.013 --> 00:02:28.348
Lucia typed pwd and read slash users slash Lucia, her personal

58
00:02:28.373 --> 00:02:29.193
folder.

59
00:02:29.413 --> 00:02:31.273
Nothing was created or deleted.

60
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:33.893
Once you know where you are, look at what's there.

61
00:02:33.933 --> 00:02:37.208
The ls command lists the folders and files in the current

62
00:02:37.233 --> 00:02:38.028
place.

63
00:02:38.053 --> 00:02:40.508
It shows what a program will see if it starts working

64
00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:40.993
from there.

65
00:02:42.413 --> 00:02:46.048
Denise opened Codex in the downloads folder while testing something

66
00:02:46.073 --> 00:02:49.588
for a colleague, and it couldn't see her project which was

67
00:02:49.613 --> 00:02:50.668
in another folder.

68
00:02:50.693 --> 00:02:53.808
A PWD would have shown that beforehand.

69
00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:57.448
To change folders, there's capital, which we will leave for lesson

70
00:02:57.473 --> 00:02:58.353
16.

71
00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:00.388
Now pause the video.

72
00:03:00.413 --> 00:03:03.628
Open the terminal, type pound and write down the folder you

73
00:03:03.653 --> 00:03:04.788
get.

74
00:03:04.813 --> 00:03:07.500
Then type ls and write down three names.

75
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.555
On the Mac, you'll see names like documents.

76
00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.815
On a freshly installed Ubuntu, the list may be empty.

77
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:14.415
And that's normal.

78
00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.500
If you see command not found, check your typing.

79
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.200
All lowercase, no space in the middle.

80
00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.840
Now let's put Codex on the computer.

81
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:24.975
Codex is the program that will work inside the terminal, in

82
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.320
a folder of yours.

83
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.900
First, a distinction that avoids a lot of confusion.

84
00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:33.275
Putting the program on the computer, signing in with your account,

85
00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.975
and opening the program in the project folder are three different

86
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:36.340
steps.

87
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.695
Each with its own check.

88
00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.960
This lesson does only the first one.

89
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.715
The account comes in lesson 15 and the folder in lesson

90
00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:45.140
16.

91
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.635
Right after installing, Lucia typed codex and the program asked her

92
00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:51.120
to sign in with her account.

93
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.175
She thought it was a bug.

94
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.175
It wasn't.

95
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.280
The first step had worked, and the second was still missing.

96
00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.915
When you separate the steps, each new message stops looking like

97
00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.775
an error and starts telling you where you are along the

98
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.020
way.

99
00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:09.040
Faster commands for installing programs circulate on the internet.

100
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.640
But a command pasted without checking can run anything.

101
00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.960
Think of a package that arrives at the school office.

102
00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.280
Before opening it, you look at the seal and the sender.

103
00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.140
A command works the same way.

104
00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.660
And the sender is the address it comes from.

105
00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.815
The official codex command downloads a script from the chatgpt.com

106
00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.260
address and runs that script.

107
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.220
That's why the address matters so much.

108
00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.535
Denise got a faster way to install codecs in a group

109
00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:38.300
chat.

110
00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:40.815
The address in the middle of the command had codecs in

111
00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:44.000
its name, but it wasn't the one from the official page.

112
00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.195
She didn't paste it and use the one from the page.

113
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.580
So, the rule is simple.

114
00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.575
You don't paste a command from an unknown page and you

115
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:56.255
read the address inside the command, not just the title of

116
00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:56.755
the message.

117
00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:01.015
On MacOS and Linux, the command from the official page starts

118
00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.735
with curl, which downloads the file from the address, and hands

119
00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:07.135
that file to sha, which runs it.

120
00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:11.284
On Windows with WSL, you paste the same command inside Ubuntu.

121
00:05:11.309 --> 00:05:14.144
The installer prints a few lines and you don't need to

122
00:05:14.169 --> 00:05:17.629
understand each one, wait for the dollar sign to come back.

123
00:05:17.789 --> 00:05:21.024
Then ask for the version with the command codec's dash dash

124
00:05:21.049 --> 00:05:21.444
version.

125
00:05:21.469 --> 00:05:24.984
If you see command not found, the terminal you opened earlier

126
00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:27.049
doesn't know about the new program yet.

127
00:05:27.169 --> 00:05:28.564
That's what happened to Denise.

128
00:05:28.589 --> 00:05:32.124
She closed the terminal, opened another one and the number showed

129
00:05:32.149 --> 00:05:32.689
up.

130
00:05:32.809 --> 00:05:33.484
Pause the video.

131
00:05:33.509 --> 00:05:36.584
Open the official Codex page, check that the command is the

132
00:05:36.609 --> 00:05:41.764
same, paste it, wait, reopen the terminal and write down the

133
00:05:41.789 --> 00:05:43.109
version number.

134
00:05:43.469 --> 00:05:46.724
If the computer belongs to the school and asks for a

135
00:05:46.749 --> 00:05:49.964
password, stop and talk to whoever manages it.

136
00:05:49.989 --> 00:05:53.444
Codex is on the computer, but it doesn't know who you

137
00:05:53.469 --> 00:05:53.924
are yet.

138
00:05:53.949 --> 00:05:57.724
At school, you swipe your badge at the turnstile and nobody

139
00:05:57.749 --> 00:05:59.069
hears your password.

140
00:05:59.189 --> 00:06:01.729
The Codex login works the same way.

141
00:06:01.789 --> 00:06:06.384
You type codex login, the terminal sends you to the browser,

142
00:06:06.409 --> 00:06:09.304
and that's where you sign in with your ChatGPT account.

143
00:06:09.329 --> 00:06:13.354
When you finish, Codex gets the confirmation and saves the sign-in.

144
00:06:13.379 --> 00:06:16.159
The password never goes through the terminal.

145
00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:17.954
Lúcia did exactly that.

146
00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:22.834
She typed codexlogin, the browser opened on the ChatGPT sign-in screen,

147
00:06:22.859 --> 00:06:25.174
she signed in with the school account and went back to

148
00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:25.694
the terminal.

149
00:06:25.719 --> 00:06:28.254
One precaution before you start.

150
00:06:28.279 --> 00:06:32.979
Which ChatGPT plans include Codex changes over time?

151
00:06:33.219 --> 00:06:35.934
Check the official authentication page to see if your plan is

152
00:06:35.959 --> 00:06:36.114
there.

153
00:06:36.139 --> 00:06:38.974
The plan name appears in your account settings.

154
00:06:38.999 --> 00:06:40.714
Being connected isn't enough.

155
00:06:40.739 --> 00:06:43.579
What matters is which path you took.

156
00:06:43.979 --> 00:06:47.234
Signing in with the account uses that account's plan.

157
00:06:47.259 --> 00:06:50.754
An API key is built by usage, on a different account.

158
00:06:50.779 --> 00:06:52.534
It's that difference from lesson 5.

159
00:06:52.559 --> 00:06:54.294
Two accounts, two bills.

160
00:06:54.319 --> 00:06:58.979
To find out the path, use the command codex login status.

161
00:06:59.019 --> 00:07:02.854
With the account, the answer is logged in using chat GPT.

162
00:07:02.879 --> 00:07:06.114
With a key, it mentions the key, but never the full

163
00:07:06.139 --> 00:07:06.874
value.

164
00:07:06.899 --> 00:07:10.814
Denise expected to see chat GPT and found out Codex was

165
00:07:10.839 --> 00:07:13.034
signing in with an old-school API key.

166
00:07:13.059 --> 00:07:16.443
She logged out with Codex Logout and signed in again with

167
00:07:16.468 --> 00:07:18.308
Codex Login, using the account.

168
00:07:19.528 --> 00:07:23.003
So, read that answer before asking for any long task.

169
00:07:23.028 --> 00:07:26.108
It's what tells you which account the usage comes from.

170
00:07:26.948 --> 00:07:30.703
If you're signing in with your ChatGPT account, you can skip

171
00:07:30.728 --> 00:07:31.683
this part.

172
00:07:31.708 --> 00:07:34.423
People who use the API keep the key under a name,

173
00:07:34.448 --> 00:07:39.583
the terminal knows, OpenAI API key and the official command hands

174
00:07:39.608 --> 00:07:43.428
that value straight to Codex without showing it on the screen.

175
00:07:43.908 --> 00:07:46.908
The common mistake is typing the whole key into the command.

176
00:07:46.968 --> 00:07:50.228
It stays in the history and shows up in any screenshot.

177
00:07:50.588 --> 00:07:54.048
And a .environ file on its own doesn't connect anything.

178
00:07:54.108 --> 00:07:57.263
When a colleague asked Lucia for the school's key, she didn't

179
00:07:57.288 --> 00:07:58.668
send it through the group.

180
00:07:58.708 --> 00:08:00.868
Each person signs in with their own account.

181
00:08:02.328 --> 00:08:03.368
Pause the video.

182
00:08:03.568 --> 00:08:07.003
Type codex login, sign in on the browser, and then run

183
00:08:07.028 --> 00:08:08.603
codex login status.

184
00:08:08.628 --> 00:08:12.603
On the project sheet, write a line like the NISIS, codex

185
00:08:12.628 --> 00:08:16.603
chatgpt, checked with codex login status, make sure there's no password

186
00:08:16.628 --> 00:08:17.648
at all on that line.

187
00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.980
Codex is connected, but which folder will it work in?

188
00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:22.800
It works in the folder where it was opened.

189
00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:26.375
In the wrong folder, it reads what it shouldn't, and can't

190
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:27.155
find what it needs.

191
00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:30.015
If you walk into the wrong room, you teach the wrong

192
00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:33.700
class, so you go into the folder before opening the program.

193
00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:35.155
Three commands do this.

194
00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:40.575
markdir-p creates the folder, and the ones before it too, if

195
00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:43.080
they're missing, capital goes into it.

196
00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.655
And pwd confirms the address.

197
00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:46.115
address.

198
00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.535
The tilde sign means my personal folder, the one with your

199
00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:52.900
name and the little house icon in the Mac Finder.

200
00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:56.715
Denise created the training folder, meu primeiro projeto, and went

201
00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:57.595
into it.

202
00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:01.155
The first two comments don't answer anything when they work.

203
00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:05.555
What confirmed the address, before she opened any program, was parties.

204
00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.095
You don't need to open your entire personal folder to try

205
00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:08.755
this out.

206
00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.055
A small area with only the material for the task reduces

207
00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.495
confusion, and when something goes wrong, it's clear which files could

208
00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:16.695
have changed.

209
00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.915
Lucia thought about opening codecs in her Documents folder, where

210
00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:21.775
her classes' tests and grades are.

211
00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:26.036
She chose the training folder instead, with a fictional agenda.

212
00:09:26.061 --> 00:09:28.376
Nothing real was within reach.

213
00:09:28.401 --> 00:09:29.956
Three things go inside it.

214
00:09:29.981 --> 00:09:34.556
The readme describes the purpose of the project for whoever arrives.

215
00:09:34.581 --> 00:09:39.176
Agents.md gives the agent its working instructions.

216
00:09:39.201 --> 00:09:42.996
Both end in .md because they are marked down, plain text, where

217
00:09:43.021 --> 00:09:45.796
the hash sign marks the title and the hyphen marks the

218
00:09:45.821 --> 00:09:47.096
list item.

219
00:09:47.121 --> 00:09:49.656
Denise put two rules in the agents.md column.

220
00:09:49.681 --> 00:09:53.181
Work only in that folder and don't send anything.

221
00:09:54.201 --> 00:09:57.816
And the Entradas folder holds the fictional meeting agenda.

222
00:09:57.841 --> 00:10:01.236
Before typing codex, run pound and liverment.

223
00:10:01.261 --> 00:10:04.576
If the address and the files match, open the program there.

224
00:10:04.601 --> 00:10:08.036
The first time in a folder, codex asks whether you trust

225
00:10:08.061 --> 00:10:09.681
it.

226
00:10:09.901 --> 00:10:13.456
because there it can read, edit and run files within your

227
00:10:13.481 --> 00:10:14.621
permissions.

228
00:10:14.721 --> 00:10:16.136
It's your training folder.

229
00:10:16.161 --> 00:10:19.436
Choose Trust and continue with the arrow keys and press Enter.

230
00:10:19.461 --> 00:10:22.676
In this practice, don't use Open Restricted.

231
00:10:22.701 --> 00:10:25.496
The wording may change a bit between versions.

232
00:10:25.521 --> 00:10:28.716
To exit, type slash quit.

233
00:10:28.741 --> 00:10:30.356
Now pause the video.

234
00:10:30.381 --> 00:10:34.338
Copy the practice block from the lesson, which creates the folder

235
00:10:34.363 --> 00:10:38.723
and the three fictional files, and paste it into the terminal.

236
00:10:38.983 --> 00:10:43.078
Check that pwd ends in meu primeiro projeto and that ls

237
00:10:43.103 --> 00:10:48.263
shows against .md, readme, .md and entradas.

238
00:10:48.543 --> 00:10:52.743
Then type codex, trust the folder and exit.

239
00:10:52.783 --> 00:10:54.463
With slash quit.

240
00:10:54.543 --> 00:10:56.383
Without asking for anything yet.

241
00:10:56.463 --> 00:10:58.203
Codex is in the right folder.

242
00:10:58.483 --> 00:11:00.498
What should the first request be?

243
00:11:00.523 --> 00:11:03.538
A request like fix up the project mix's diagnosis and change

244
00:11:03.563 --> 00:11:06.978
and if something goes wrong, you don't know which part it

245
00:11:07.003 --> 00:11:07.743
was.

246
00:11:07.903 --> 00:11:12.003
No serious person starts a renovation by knocking down a wall.

247
00:11:12.303 --> 00:11:13.898
First comes the inspection.

248
00:11:13.923 --> 00:11:15.678
Look, take notes, understand.

249
00:11:15.703 --> 00:11:20.463
With an agent, it's the same order, in four steps.

250
00:11:20.743 --> 00:11:24.843
Inspect, by reading and explaining, without editing anything.

251
00:11:25.823 --> 00:11:30.183
Plan, by saying what's missing, and that list becomes your reference.

252
00:11:30.543 --> 00:11:33.598
Change a single file, by name, and you're the one who

253
00:11:33.623 --> 00:11:34.538
says which.

254
00:11:34.563 --> 00:11:37.959
And validate, you open it and check, because you have the

255
00:11:37.984 --> 00:11:38.504
final word.

256
00:11:39.724 --> 00:11:42.904
Denise was going to ask Codex to improve the meeting folder.

257
00:11:43.064 --> 00:11:44.764
She switched to two requests.

258
00:11:44.844 --> 00:11:48.764
First, read and say what's missing, then create a single file.

259
00:11:48.804 --> 00:11:55.119
In the training folder, ask Codex to read agents.md and the

260
00:11:55.144 --> 00:11:56.584
readme and explain the purpose.

261
00:11:57.604 --> 00:11:58.239
Say it plainly.

262
00:11:58.264 --> 00:11:59.764
No editing.

263
00:12:00.284 --> 00:12:02.919
and ask it to name the files it used.

264
00:12:02.944 --> 00:12:07.299
Lúcia made that request, and Codex's actual answer was roughly this.

265
00:12:07.324 --> 00:12:10.959
The purpose is to prepare the agenda for a teacher's meeting.

266
00:12:10.984 --> 00:12:15.799
What's missing is the finished agenda, the output format, and location,

267
00:12:15.824 --> 00:12:17.499
and how to check the result.

268
00:12:17.524 --> 00:12:19.699
And one important detail.

269
00:12:19.724 --> 00:12:23.359
The input file exists, but I didn't read its contents.

270
00:12:23.384 --> 00:12:25.379
Nothing was edited.

271
00:12:25.404 --> 00:12:29.259
Now compare the list of what it used with what LIS

272
00:12:29.284 --> 00:12:30.479
shows in the folder.

273
00:12:30.504 --> 00:12:33.819
It didn't open the agenda, so the list of what's missing

274
00:12:33.844 --> 00:12:35.639
comes only from the README.

275
00:12:35.664 --> 00:12:38.819
Denise read that limit and in the second request, made it

276
00:12:38.844 --> 00:12:41.100
clear that the plan had to be based on the agenda.

277
00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:43.340
Now, authorize a small change.

278
00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:46.955
Create only plano.md with three actions and a check for each

279
00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:47.180
one.

280
00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:50.080
Based on the agenda.

281
00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:52.955
Codex may ask whether you want to make the edits.

282
00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.935
If the change is only to plano.md, choose Yes, proceed.

283
00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:00.480
If it's another file, choose the option that starts with No.

284
00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:04.195
It may also create the file without asking, because the current

285
00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.615
permissions let it write in the folder.

286
00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:06.875
Check with LS.

287
00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:11.140
The plan Lucia got covered the five items on the agenda,

288
00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:15.895
and noted that times and dates still had to be set.

289
00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:20.340
She checked it against reunial.txt and everything was there.

290
00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:21.935
Pause the video.

291
00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:24.880
Make the reading request, then the plan request.

292
00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:26.800
Exit with slash quit.

293
00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:32.115
Show the file with catplano.md and check one of the checks

294
00:13:32.140 --> 00:13:34.080
directly against the agenda.

295
00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:37.260
In the last lesson, the agent created a file.

296
00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:41.100
What if it had created the wrong one or deleted another?

297
00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:43.555
Whoever is going to use the lab gets the key to

298
00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:45.840
the lab, not the whole keyring.

299
00:13:45.911 --> 00:13:47.671
With an agent, it's the same.

300
00:13:47.731 --> 00:13:50.906
Permissions control what it can reach in files, on the internet,

301
00:13:50.931 --> 00:13:51.871
and in commands.

302
00:13:52.731 --> 00:13:54.791
Go up one step at a time.

303
00:13:54.831 --> 00:13:59.086
Reading files is low risk, writing is medium risk, and running

304
00:13:59.111 --> 00:14:00.471
commands is high risk.

305
00:14:01.391 --> 00:14:02.811
And notice one difference.

306
00:14:03.331 --> 00:14:07.446
What you write in agents.md guides the agent, but it's text

307
00:14:07.471 --> 00:14:09.406
and doesn't technically prevent anything.

308
00:14:09.431 --> 00:14:12.491
What prevents things is the program's permissions.

309
00:14:12.751 --> 00:14:16.351
In Codex, the slash permissions command shows and changes those permissions.

310
00:14:17.031 --> 00:14:20.591
When Codex asked for internet access to look up some documentation,

311
00:14:20.671 --> 00:14:23.986
Lucia evaluated just that request, without granting anything else

312
00:14:24.011 --> 00:14:24.531
along with it.

313
00:14:25.611 --> 00:14:27.731
Going back requires having saved before.

314
00:14:28.311 --> 00:14:33.291
The command cp-r copies an entire folder with everything inside.

315
00:14:34.111 --> 00:14:37.766
Make the copy before the change, with a name that says

316
00:14:37.791 --> 00:14:38.446
what it is.

317
00:14:38.471 --> 00:14:43.186
Before authorizing a change to agents.md, Denis copied the training

318
00:14:43.211 --> 00:14:47.426
folder with the ending "-antes", which means before.

319
00:14:47.451 --> 00:14:50.366
Cp doesn't answer anything when it works.

320
00:14:50.391 --> 00:14:54.256
What confirmed it was Livermorium, with the two folders side by

321
00:14:54.241 --> 00:14:58.361
The folder ending in antes isn't touched by the agent.

322
00:14:58.581 --> 00:15:00.261
It's your return point.

323
00:15:00.541 --> 00:15:03.496
Later on, in module 5, Git does this in a more

324
00:15:03.521 --> 00:15:04.721
complete way.

325
00:15:04.981 --> 00:15:07.776
For now, a simple copy does the job, and don't remove

326
00:15:07.801 --> 00:15:10.376
all of the program's protections to get around an error.

327
00:15:10.401 --> 00:15:14.601
Start with the most restrictive setting that can handle the task.

328
00:15:14.681 --> 00:15:18.636
After the change, the command diff-r compares the copy with the

329
00:15:18.661 --> 00:15:22.021
current folder and shows only the differences, file by file.

330
00:15:22.501 --> 00:15:25.461
The output looks like code, but you only need two things.

331
00:15:26.281 --> 00:15:29.296
Which files show up, and which lines start with the greater

332
00:15:29.321 --> 00:15:31.421
than sign, which are the new ones.

333
00:15:31.661 --> 00:15:35.796
In Lucia's diff, a single file showed up, against.md, with two

334
00:15:35.821 --> 00:15:36.321
new lines.

335
00:15:36.941 --> 00:15:38.701
That was exactly what she had authorized.

336
00:15:39.621 --> 00:15:42.416
If another file shows up, the antes copy has the old

337
00:15:42.441 --> 00:15:43.961
version for you to restore.

338
00:15:44.581 --> 00:15:47.756
Pause the video, make the copy, open codecs in the training

339
00:15:47.781 --> 00:15:51.916
folder, look at slash permissions without changing anything and ask

340
00:15:51.941 --> 00:15:57.427
for two new lines in agents.md, without changing anything else.

341
00:15:57.452 --> 00:15:59.187
Exit and run the diff.

342
00:15:59.212 --> 00:16:03.227
You're done when only agents.md shows up, with the two new

343
00:16:03.252 --> 00:16:04.307
lines.

344
00:16:04.332 --> 00:16:05.847
Let's put it all together.

345
00:16:05.872 --> 00:16:09.767
You open the terminal and ask where you are with pwd,

346
00:16:09.792 --> 00:16:11.987
and what's there with ls.

347
00:16:12.012 --> 00:16:16.027
You install codecs from the official source, reading the address inside

348
00:16:16.052 --> 00:16:18.572
the command, and check it with the version.

349
00:16:18.672 --> 00:16:22.547
You sign in with your account through the browser, check the

350
00:16:22.572 --> 00:16:26.587
method with codex login status and write only the method on

351
00:16:26.612 --> 00:16:28.207
the sheet, never the key.

352
00:16:28.232 --> 00:16:31.767
You open the agent in a small folder with only the

353
00:16:31.792 --> 00:16:32.647
material for the task.

354
00:16:32.672 --> 00:16:35.687
You ask first for a reading, then for a change by

355
00:16:35.712 --> 00:16:38.947
name and check it against the real material.

356
00:16:38.972 --> 00:16:42.727
And before changing anything, you save a copy and compare with

357
00:16:42.752 --> 00:16:42.947
DIV.

358
00:16:42.972 --> 00:16:46.647
When you have about 30 minutes, do the module lab, first

359
00:16:46.672 --> 00:16:47.932
guided project.

360
00:16:48.292 --> 00:16:50.667
the supplementary material for lesson 18.

361
00:16:50.692 --> 00:16:53.767
In the next module, folders, markdowns and secrets.

362
00:16:53.792 --> 00:16:57.407
The project's digital home, with instruction files and a separate

363
00:16:57.432 --> 00:16:58.832
place for passwords.

364
00:16:59.692 --> 00:17:00.702
See you there!
